  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1228 PM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nPWATs will continue to climb and set new calendar day records\nSunday as the atmospheric river is steered over NM in advance of\nthe approaching upper level trough/low. High snow levels on Sunday\nof 11Kft or higher will bring a rain-on-snow component to this\nevent, mainly across the San Juan Mountains of Colorado where high\nqpf and significant runoff is expected to route down the La Plata\nand Animas Rivers and end up in the San Juan River. River rises\nare a guarantee and there is a moderate to high chance the\naforementioned rivers go to at least minor flood stage. That\nprocess will take some time and river flooding may not materialize\nin our forecast area until Sunday night or Monday. The San\nFrancisco River in Catron County is also at risk of flooding as\nupslope forcing hits the far eastern extent of the Mogollon rim \nhard. There is also a lesser flash flood risk due to lower \nrainfall rates, certainly relative to last week, as this event \nquickly becomes orographically forced on Sunday. Any urban or \narroyo flooding will take longer to develop. This is all covered \nin our current Flood Watch, which goes into effect Sunday morning \nand continues through Monday morning. There is also a low risk of\nsevere thunderstorms Sunday night across eastern NM, mainly \nacross the east central and southeast plains near the TX border \nwhere 0-6km bulk shear may exceed 60kts and sufficient, although \nunimpressive, instability will linger ahead of a Pacific cold \nfront. Strong winds are forecast both ahead of and behind the cold\nfront going into Monday and a Wind Advisory may be required for \ncentral and northeast portions of the area. Snow levels will come \ndown Sunday night through Monday as the cold front pushes through \nand the upper low moves over northern NM, bringing a few inches of\nsnow to the northern mountains above 9Kft. Stronger northerly \nwinds will likely persist behind the cold front on the backside of\nthe departing upper low across far easter NM Monday night through\nTuesday. A warming/drying trend is forecast thereafter, although \nthe ridging going into mid week is more transient with the latest \nmodeling. Significant model spread from mid through late week is \nleading to a lower confidence forecast.",
